-- title:
--   Theorem 3.4: existence and uniqueness of a time-consistent dynamic distortion function on a binomial tree
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $X$ is the recombining binomial tree of (3.11): nodes $x_{i,0}<\dots<x_{i,i}$ at time $t_i$ ($0\le i\le N$), up-probabilities $0<p^+_{i,j}<1$; $\varphi_{t_1},\dots,\varphi_{t_N}$ are distortion functions and $\varphi_{t_0}(p)=p$; $G_{i,j}=\mathbb P(X_{t_i}\ge x_{i,j})$, $G_{i,i+1}=0$; $q^\pm_{i,j}$ and $\mathbb Q$ are given by (3.13) and (3.15); and condition (3.14) holds.
--
--   Then:
--
--   1. **Existence.** There is a time-consistent dynamic distortion function $\Phi$ such that $\Phi(t_0,t_n,x_{0,0};p)=\varphi_{t_n}(p)$ for $n=1,\dots,N$ and all $p\in[0,1]$, and for all $0\le i<n\le N$, $0\le j\le i$ and $0\le k\le n$,
--   $$
--   \Phi\bigl(t_i,t_n,x_{i,j};\mathbb P\{X_{t_n}\ge x_{n,k}\mid X_{t_i}=x_{i,j}\}\bigr)=\mathbb Q\{X_{t_n}\ge x_{n,k}\mid X_{t_i}=x_{i,j}\}; \tag{3.17}
--   $$
--   moreover its conditional nonlinear expectation satisfies
--   $$
--   \mathcal E_{t_i,t_n}[g(X_{t_n})]=\mathbb E^{\mathbb Q}\bigl[g(X_{t_n})\mid\sigma(X_{t_i})\bigr]\qquad\text{for all }0\le i<n\le N,\ g\in\mathcal I. \tag{3.12}
--   $$
--   2. **Uniqueness at the conditional survival probabilities.** Every time-consistent dynamic distortion function $\Psi$ with the same initial condition satisfies (3.17), and its conditional nonlinear expectation satisfies (3.12).
--
--   The theorem produces a conditional expectation under probability distortion that agrees with the distorted expectation $\mathcal E$ at time $0$ (through the initial condition) and satisfies the tower property; it is the linear conditional expectation under the distorted probability $\mathbb Q$ for every payoff in $\mathcal I$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The model is the canonical path space of the tree; times are indices; $\mathcal F_{t_i}$ is generated by the paths' first $i$ moves; identities between random variables are stated at every path. $\Psi$ in clause 2 is not assumed to satisfy (3.17).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a Markov process X on an abstract filtered probability space with transition law (3.11) and the filtration F = F^X it generates → the canonical path space Fin N → Bool (true = up) with path weights ∏ p^±, and X_{t_i}(ω) = x_{i, number of up-moves before i}; reason: every quantity in the statement depends only on the law of X and on F^X; the same instances
--   - encoding: times 0 = t_0 < ⋯ < t_N → time indices 0, …, N (Φ(t_i, t_n, x; p) is Φ i n x p, φ_{t_n} is φ n); reason: the times enter only as labels
--   - encoding: the assumption φ_0(p) := p → phiExt φ 0 = id built into the definitions; the value φ 0 of the given family is ignored; reason: the same convention, without a hypothesis on φ 0
--   - encoding: conditioning on F_{t_i} (resp. σ(X_{t_i})) and identities between random variables → conditioning on the atom of F_{t_i} containing ω (resp. on the event {X_{t_i} = X_{t_i}(ω)}), identities stated at every path ω; reason: F_{t_i} = σ(X_{t_0}, …, X_{t_i}) is generated by these atoms, and every path has positive probability, so almost-sure identities are identities at every path
--   - encoding: ∫₀^∞ Φ(s, t, X_s; ℙ(ξ ≥ x | F_s)) dx → Bochner integral over (0, ∞) of the same integrand; reason: the integrand is bounded with bounded support for every ξ the statement uses, so the integral is finite and equals the paper's
--   - encoding: Φ jointly Lebesgue measurable in (x, p) → NullMeasurable for the Lebesgue measure of ℝ × [0, 1] (volume on ℝ × ℝ restricted to univ ×ˢ Icc 0 1); reason: null-measurability for Lebesgue measure is Lebesgue measurability
--   - reading: "there exists a unique time-consistent dynamic distortion function Φ such that [initial condition] and (3.17) … uniqueness is only at the conditional survival probabilities" → every time-consistent dynamic distortion function Ψ with the initial condition satisfies (3.17) and (3.12); Ψ is not assumed to satisfy (3.17); reason: the proof (pp.14–15) starts from "an arbitrary time-consistent dynamic distortion function" and derives (3.17) and (3.12); this is the stronger of the two readings
-- source:
--   Ma–Wong–Zhang, Time-consistent conditional expectation under probability distortion, arXiv:1809.08262v5, https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08262v5, Theorem 3.4, pp.13–14 (with eqs. (3.12), p.12, and (3.17), p.13)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DistortedCondExp_Distortion
import Definitions.Def_DistortedCondExp_Tree
import Definitions.Def_DistortedCondExp_Dynamic

open MeasureTheory Set

namespace DistortedCondExp

/-- **Theorem 3.4** (Ma–Wong–Zhang, *Time-consistent conditional expectation under probability
distortion*, arXiv:1809.08262v5, pp.13–14). Let `X` be the recombining binomial tree (3.11) with
nodes `x_{i,0} < ⋯ < x_{i,i}` and up-probabilities `0 < p⁺_{i,j} < 1`, let `φ_{t_1}, …, φ_{t_N}` be
distortion functions, `φ_{t_0} := id`, and assume (3.14). Then there exists a unique
time-consistent dynamic distortion function `Φ` such that `Φ(t_0, t_n, x_{0,0}; p) = φ_{t_n}(p)` for
`n = 1, …, N`, and for all `0 ≤ i < n ≤ N`, `0 ≤ j ≤ i`, and `0 ≤ k ≤ n`,
`Φ(t_i, t_n, x_{i,j}; ℙ{X_{t_n} ≥ x_{n,k} | X_{t_i} = x_{i,j}}) = ℚ{X_{t_n} ≥ x_{n,k} | X_{t_i} = x_{i,j}}`
(3.17). Here uniqueness is only at the conditional survival probabilities for all `k` in the left
side of (3.17). Moreover, the corresponding conditional nonlinear expectation satisfies (3.12).

**Formalization Note** The first conjunct is existence: a time-consistent dynamic distortion
function with the initial condition (on all of `[0, 1]`), (3.17) and (3.12). The second is
uniqueness at the conditional survival probabilities, in the reading of the proof (p.14: "Assume
`Φ` is an arbitrary time-consistent dynamic distortion function"): every time-consistent dynamic
distortion function with the initial condition satisfies (3.17), and its conditional nonlinear
expectation satisfies (3.12). `ℚ` is the law (3.15) with `q⁺` of (3.13). Times are indices, the
model is the canonical path space, `F_{t_i}` is generated by the atoms `histAtom i ω`. -/
theorem theorem_3_4 (N : ℕ) (x p : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (φ : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ)
    (htree : IsBinomialTree N x p) (hφ : ∀ n, 1 ≤ n → n ≤ N → IsDistortion (φ n))
    (h314 : Cond314 N x p φ) :
    (∃ Φ : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ, IsDynamicDistortion N Φ ∧ IsTimeConsistent N x p Φ ∧
      InitialCondition N x φ Φ ∧ Satisfies317 N x p φ Φ ∧ Satisfies312 N x p φ Φ) ∧
    ∀ Ψ : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ, IsDynamicDistortion N Ψ → IsTimeConsistent N x p Ψ →
      InitialCondition N x φ Ψ → Satisfies317 N x p φ Ψ ∧ Satisfies312 N x p φ Ψ := by sorry

end DistortedCondExp
